      "id": 1,
      "text": "Bladensburg was originally called Garrison's Landing before being renamed for Thomas Bladen, governor of Maryland from 1742 to 1747.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bladensburg%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Battle of Bladensburg in 1814 is the only U.S. battle where a sitting president, James Madison, rode into combat.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bladensburg%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Bladensburg served as the original terminus of the Baltimore and Ohio Railroad, forcing Washington passengers to transfer to carriages for the city.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bladensburg%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Bladensburg Waterfront Park sits at the confluence of the Northeast and Northwest Branches of the Anacostia River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bladensburg%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1820, naval hero Stephen Decatur was mortally wounded in a duel with James Barron in Bladensburg, even though dueling had been outlawed four years earlier.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
